# Kstack App Sidecar GraphQL schema

"An ISO-8601 encoded UTC date string."
scalar Time

"""
The identity of a persisted object (a cluster, a cluster's cache, …), opaque to
clients (a decimal-string id on the wire). One scalar for every object kind so
the same id machinery is reused. Use values returned by the server verbatim; do
not construct or parse them.
"""
scalar ObjectID

# --- Cluster (a tracked kube-context) ---

"""
A registered cluster connection: a tracked kube-context paired with its on-disk
cache mirror. Modeled Kubernetes-style so a consumer knows where data comes
from: `spec` is the user-owned desired state, `status` the observed state. Two
records may point at the same physical cluster (`status.server.uid` tracks
physical identity). A cluster stays listed after its context leaves the
kubeconfig (so its cache can still be inspected or cleared).
"""
type Cluster {
  "Unique identifier for this cluster record."
  id: ObjectID!

  "The record's spec generation, bumped on every spec write. Compare against a condition's observedGeneration to detect a stale (not-yet-reconciled) condition."
  generation: Int!
  "When this cluster was first registered."
  createdAt: Time!
  "When this cluster's deletion was requested (beehive soft-delete tombstone); null unless deletion is pending. The record lingers until its finalizers clear."
  deletionRequestedAt: Time

  "Desired state: the user-owned fields."
  spec: ClusterSpec!
  "Observed connection/health state, discovered by probing."
  status: ClusterStatus!
}

"A cluster record's desired state. Every field is user/API-owned; nothing here is observed."
type ClusterSpec {
  "User-assigned display name; null if unset."
  name: String
  "Whether the user has enabled this cluster in the app: it appears in cluster lists / context pickers and is eligible to connect. Disabled clusters stay tracked but dormant."
  enabled: Boolean!
  "Whether cache syncing is enabled. Disabling stops sync and freezes the cache. Only meaningful while enabled."
  syncEnabled: Boolean!
  "Where this record comes from and how its credentials resolve."
  source: ClusterSpecSource!
}

"""
The discriminated union naming a cluster record's origin: exactly one variant
is set. Each variant is owned by exactly one creator (the kubeconfig importer
today; manual creation and kstack-cloud sync are future variants).
"""
type ClusterSpecSource {
  "Set when the record tracks a kube-context in the user's kubeconfig."
  kubeconfig: ClusterSpecSourceKubeconfig
}

"The kubeconfig-sourced variant of ClusterSpecSource."
type ClusterSpecSourceKubeconfig {
  "The kube-context name this record tracks."
  context: String!
}

"""
A cluster record's observed connection/health state, persisted on the Cluster
record by the connection controller and reported Kubernetes-style: probed facts
plus status conditions — the consumer derives display state from the conditions'
type/status/reason. Sync status and the on-disk cache live on the `caches` children
(mirroring the beehive ClusterCache kind), not here.
"""
type ClusterStatus {
  "Per-source observation blocks, mirroring `spec.source`."
  source: ClusterStatusSource!
  "Last-known facts about the remote cluster (uid, version), discovered by probing."
  server: ClusterServer!
  "The connecting client's identity and effective access on this cluster."
  principal: ClusterPrincipal!
  "When a connection probe last succeeded; null if never."
  lastConnectedAt: Time

  "The connection controller's conditions (`Connected`, `Healthy`)."
  conditions: [Condition!]!
}

"A condition's three-valued verdict, Kubernetes-style."
enum ConditionStatus {
  "The condition holds."
  True
  "The condition does not hold."
  False
  "The condition cannot currently be assessed."
  Unknown
}

"""
One Kubernetes-style status condition: an independently-tracked aspect of the
record's observed state. Connection-controller conditions (under `status`):
`Connected` (the last probe reached the API server; reasons Connecting/
Connected/ResolveFailed/ProbeFailed/Inactive) and `Healthy` (the server's own
readiness; reasons Ready/ReadyzFailed/Unreachable/NoConnection/Inactive).
Sync-controller condition (under a cache's `status`): `Synced` (reasons Syncing/
Watching/Paused/SyncFailed/ResolveFailed). New types/reasons may appear;
consumers should treat unknown spellings as a degraded/unknown display state.
"""
type Condition {
  "The aspect this condition tracks, e.g. \"Connected\"."
  type: String!
  status: ConditionStatus!
  "CamelCase machine-readable explanation of `status`."
  reason: String!
  "Human-readable detail; empty when there is nothing to explain."
  message: String!
  "The spec generation the pass that wrote this condition observed; a gap to the record's `generation` marks it stale."
  observedGeneration: Int!
  "When `status` last changed — not when the condition was last refreshed."
  lastTransitionTime: Time!
}

"An event's severity, mirroring the control plane's event type: Normal (✓) or Warning (✗)."
enum EventType {
  Normal
  Warning
}

"""
One coalesced run from an object's control-plane event timeline: consecutive
occurrences sharing an outcome (category, type, reason) are aggregated, so
`count`/`firstAt`/`lastAt` describe the run. A generic projection served under
every kind's events surface — `category` names the timeline (e.g. `"connection"`),
`reason` is an open, category-specific machine code; consumers should treat
unknown spellings as a degraded/unknown state. The client upserts a live stream
by `id` (a re-delivered id is an updated run; a new id is a new run).
"""
type Event {
  "Stable run identity — the client's upsert key on a watch. An opaque int64 id, same wire form as ObjectID."
  id: ObjectID!
  "The timeline this run belongs to, e.g. `\"connection\"`."
  category: String!
  type: EventType!
  "CamelCase machine code, e.g. `Connected` / `ProbeFailed`. Open-ended per category."
  reason: String!
  "The latest occurrence's detail; empty when there is nothing to explain."
  message: String!
  "Occurrences coalesced into this run (>= 1)."
  count: Int!
  "When the run started (first occurrence)."
  firstAt: Time!
  "When the run last extended (latest occurrence)."
  lastAt: Time!
}

"""
An object's reconcile-schedule gauge: when the control plane has queued the
object's next reconcile (a cluster's next backoff retry, for a disconnected one),
which a UI counts down to. Generic like Event — the watch entrypoints are
kind-scoped (clusterScheduleWatch) but the value shape is not. A struct (not a
bare Time) so it can grow. Observed live via a schedule watch — a scheduling
change fires no object/list watch, so this is the only way to keep the countdown
moving for an otherwise-idle object.
"""
type Schedule {
  "The scheduled time of the next reconcile, or null when nothing is scheduled."
  nextRequeueAt: Time
  """
  Whether a connection probe is running right now. Asserted by the controller for
  the in-flight window (not inferred from a missing nextRequeueAt), so the UI can
  show a definite "checking now" while the probe's round-trips are in flight.
  """
  probing: Boolean!
}

"""
A cache record, mirroring the beehive ClusterCache kind: a mirror of one physical
cluster identity. Streamed independently of its parent Cluster via
`clusterCachesWatch` and joined to it client-side by `clusterID`. Carries its
identity (`id`, `clusterID`, `serverUid`), observed sync `status`, and live on-disk
`stats` (a nested object so a consumer fetches the stat-querying stats only when it
selects them). A cluster owns one per identity it has mirrored; the *active* cache
— the one a sync engine runs for and a UI shows for the row — is the one whose
`serverUid` matches the cluster's last-probed `status.server.uid`, a join the
client derives (it is not a field here, because it depends on the parent's status,
which changes with no cache event and so cannot be kept fresh on this stream).
"""
type ClusterCache {
  "Unique identifier for this cache record (distinct from its parent cluster's id)."
  id: ObjectID!
  "The id of the Cluster this cache belongs to (its beehive owner). The client joins caches onto clusters by this id."
  clusterID: ObjectID!
  "The kube-system namespace UID of the physical cluster this cache mirrors — the identity a physical migration turns over. Matches the parent cluster's `status.server.uid` while this is the active cache."
  serverUid: String!
  "The sync controller's status block: its conditions (`Synced`) plus cache freshness."
  status: ClusterCacheStatus!
  "Live on-disk cache statistics, queried on demand."
  stats: ClusterCacheStats!
}

"The sync controller's status block on the cache child."
type ClusterCacheStatus {
  "The sync-owned conditions (`Synced`)."
  conditions: [Condition!]!
  "When the cache last received fresh data; null if never. Refreshed coarsely (~30s) while syncing."
  lastSyncedAt: Time
}

"""
The status-side counterpart of ClusterSpecSource: the per-source observation
blocks, of which exactly the spec source variant's is set. Null blocks mean
"this record's source has no such observation" — never that an observation
went away (an orphaned kubeconfig record keeps its block with
`isPresent: false`).
"""
type ClusterStatusSource {
  "Kubeconfig observation; null unless the record is kubeconfig-sourced."
  kubeconfig: ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfig
}

"""
A kubeconfig-sourced record's last-known kubeconfig observation: the cluster
and user names its context (`spec.source.kubeconfig.context`) references, and
whether the context is present. Cached from the last time the context was
present, so they survive the context leaving the kubeconfig and let the UI
identify an orphaned record. In a malformed kubeconfig the referenced entries
may not resolve.
"""
type ClusterStatusSourceKubeconfig {
  "Name of the cluster entry the context references; cached."
  cluster: String!
  "Name of the user (authInfo) entry the context references; cached."
  user: String!
  "True while the context is currently present in the kubeconfig; false when orphaned."
  isPresent: Boolean!
  "True when the context is the kubeconfig's `current-context`; false otherwise."
  isDefault: Boolean!
}

"Last-known facts about the remote cluster, discovered by probing. Fields are null until a successful probe."
type ClusterServer {
  "The cluster's `kube-system` namespace UID; stable across context renames."
  uid: String
  "Server version, e.g. \"v1.29.3\"."
  version: String
}

"The connecting client's identity and effective access on this cluster."
type ClusterPrincipal {
  "Authenticated username the API server last mapped this cluster's credentials to (SelfSubjectReview); null until probed."
  username: String
  "Effective RBAC in a namespace (live SelfSubjectRulesReview), resolved on demand. Point-in-time, for debugging only — not authoritative for access decisions."
  permissions(namespace: String!): ClusterPermissions!
}

"""
The connecting principal's effective RBAC in one namespace (live
SelfSubjectRulesReview). Namespace-scoped (the API has no all-namespaces form)
and point-in-time — for UI/debugging, not authoritative for access decisions.
"""
type ClusterPermissions {
  "The namespace these rules were evaluated for."
  namespace: String!
  "Allowed resource rules (verbs × apiGroups × resources)."
  resourceRules: [ResourceRule!]!
  "Allowed non-resource rules (verbs × URLs), e.g. /healthz."
  nonResourceRules: [NonResourceRule!]!
  "True if some authorizers couldn't be evaluated, so the rule set may be incomplete."
  incomplete: Boolean!
}

"A resource-scoped RBAC rule: the verbs the principal may use on the given apiGroups/resources (optionally narrowed to named instances)."
type ResourceRule {
  verbs: [String!]!
  apiGroups: [String!]!
  resources: [String!]!
  "Specific resource names this rule is restricted to; empty means all."
  resourceNames: [String!]!
}

"A non-resource RBAC rule: the verbs the principal may use against non-resource URLs (e.g. /healthz)."
type NonResourceRule {
  verbs: [String!]!
  nonResourceUrls: [String!]!
}

"Live on-disk cache statistics for a single cluster."
type ClusterCacheStats {
  "True when a local cache exists on disk."
  exists: Boolean!
  "On-disk cache size (sqlite + WAL/shm) in bytes; 0 when not cached."
  bytes: Int!
  "Total number of cached objects across every kind (the sum of the kind catalog's per-kind counts; excludes cached events). 0 when not cached."
  objectCount: Int!
  "Number of distinct kinds with at least one cached object. 0 when not cached."
  kindCount: Int!
}

"""
One kind a cluster's API server advertises, from `/apis` discovery (built-ins +
CRDs). The dashboard builds its dynamic resource nav from these: `resource` dedupes
against the curated catalog and `apiVersion`'s group buckets the kind into a nav
group.
"""
type ClusterDataKind {
  "Group/version, e.g. \"apps/v1\", or \"v1\" for the core group."
  apiVersion: String!
  "Kind name, e.g. \"Deployment\"."
  kind: String!
  "Plural lowercase resource name (the URL form), e.g. \"deployments\"."
  resource: String!
  "\"Namespaced\" or \"Cluster\"."
  scope: String!
  "True when this kind is backed by a CustomResourceDefinition."
  isCRD: Boolean!
  "Number of objects of this kind currently in the cache (0 for an advertised-but-empty kind)."
  count: Int!
}

# --- Delta watches ---

"""
Classifies a delta-watch change, mirroring a Kubernetes watch event. On subscribe a
watch replays the current set as `Added` changes (the snapshot), then streams live
changes. A `Deleted` change carries the object's last-known state; the client keys
on its `id`.
"""
enum ChangeType {
  Added
  Modified
  Deleted
}

"One change on the `clustersWatch` stream: the affected cluster and what happened to it."
type ClusterChange {
  "Whether this cluster was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified, or deleted."
  type: ChangeType!
  "The affected cluster. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; the client removes it by `id`."
  cluster: Cluster!
}

"One change on the `clusterCachesWatch` stream: the affected cache and what happened to it."
type ClusterCacheChange {
  "Whether this cache was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified, or deleted."
  type: ChangeType!
  "The affected cache. On `Deleted`, its last-known state; the client removes it by `id`."
  cache: ClusterCache!
}

"One change on the `clusterDataKindsWatch` stream: the affected kind, what happened to it, and which cache it came from."
type ClusterDataKindChange {
  "Whether this kind was added (incl. the on-subscribe snapshot), modified (chiefly a `count` change), or deleted."
  type: ChangeType!
  "The affected kind. On `Deleted`, its last-known row; the client keys on `apiVersion` + `resource`."
  kind: ClusterDataKind!
  """
  The cache this frame came from — the frame's provenance. A client watching the
  active cache rejects a late frame from a superseded cache (one still draining
  after a cache/context switch) by comparing this against the cache it subscribed for.
  """
  cacheID: ObjectID!
}

# --- Chat ---

input ChatMessageInput {
  role: String!
  content: String!
}

input ChatInput {
  messages: [ChatMessageInput!]!
}

"Streaming chat chunk. `delta` is the next piece of assistant text; `done` is true on the final frame (delta may be empty)."
type ChatChunk {
  delta: String!
  done: Boolean!
}

# --- Cloud account ---

"The verified identity of the signed-in user, from the OIDC ID-token claims."
type Identity {
  sub: String!
  email: String!
  name: String!
}

"The user's authentication state. `authenticated` is the sign-in signal; `identity` carries the verified claims and is non-null only when authenticated."
type AuthState {
  authenticated: Boolean!
  identity: Identity
}

# --- Root operations ---

type Query {
  "A single cluster by id; null if not tracked."
  cluster(id: ObjectID!): Cluster

  "Every tracked cluster — those in the kubeconfig plus any with a leftover cache. Sorted by name."
  clusters: [Cluster!]!

  """
  A cluster's control-plane event timeline (newest run first), optionally filtered
  to one `category` (e.g. `"connection"`) and bounded by `limit`. Empty for an
  unknown cluster or one with no events. Decoupled from the cluster/list watch —
  event chatter never re-emits the cluster.
  """
  clusterEvents(id: ObjectID!, category: String, limit: Int): [Event!]!

  """
  A ClusterCache's control-plane event timeline (newest run first), the
  ClusterCache-kind counterpart of `clusterEvents` — keyed by the cache's own `id`
  and optionally filtered to one `category` (e.g. `"sync"`, the cache-sync history).
  Empty for an unknown cache or one with no events. Decoupled from the cache/list
  watch.
  """
  clusterCacheEvents(id: ObjectID!, category: String, limit: Int): [Event!]!

  """
  The kinds a cluster's API server advertises (from `/apis` discovery), read from one
  ClusterCache's catalog (`id` = parent cluster, `cacheID` = the cache — typically the
  active one). Used to build the dashboard's dynamic resource nav. Empty for a cache
  that has not synced yet. Read on demand (per active cache), not streamed.
  """
  clusterDataKinds(id: ObjectID!, cacheID: ObjectID!): [ClusterDataKind!]!

  "Current authentication state. Signed-out when no cloud account is configured; live updates via authStateWatch."
  authState: AuthState!
}

type Mutation {
  # --- Cluster mutations ---

  "Enable or disable a cluster in the app. Disabling keeps the record tracked but dormant (no connection, hidden from context pickers). Returns the updated cluster."
  clusterEnabledSet(id: ObjectID!, enabled: Boolean!): Cluster!

  "Enable or disable syncing for a cluster. Disabling stops sync and freezes the cache. Returns the updated cluster."
  clusterSyncEnabledSet(id: ObjectID!, syncEnabled: Boolean!): Cluster!

  "Force an immediate reconnection attempt, resetting any backoff. Returns true once scheduled."
  clusterConnectionRetry(id: ObjectID!): Boolean!

  "Clear a cluster's on-disk cache but keep its registry record; a present, enabled cluster re-syncs from scratch. Returns the updated cluster."
  clusterCacheClear(id: ObjectID!): Cluster!

  "Forget a cluster: delete its cache and its registry record. A still-present context is re-discovered on the next kubeconfig reconcile. Returns true."
  clusterDelete(id: ObjectID!): Boolean!

  # --- Auth mutations ---

  "Start the sidecar-owned browser sign-in (OAuth). Setup (loopback bind + browser open) runs synchronously, so a setup failure — or no cloud account — returns an error. Returns true once the browser opens; the token exchange then completes in the background, with the signed-in state delivered via authStateWatch."
  authLoginStart: Boolean!

  "Sign out: clear local credentials, broadcast signed-out, and revoke the token server-side (best-effort). Returns true; no-op without a cloud account."
  authLogout: Boolean!
}

type Subscription {
  """
  Streams the cluster list as a Kubernetes-style delta watch: the current set as an
  `Added` burst on subscribe (the snapshot), then one `Added`/`Modified`/`Deleted`
  change per cluster as the registry changes. The client maintains a map keyed by
  `cluster.id`, applying each change (a deletion-pending cluster arrives as
  `Deleted`). Cache sync status is *not* here — it streams separately via
  `clusterCachesWatch` and is joined client-side — so cache churn never re-emits a
  cluster, and a consumer that only needs the cluster list (e.g. a context picker)
  is undisturbed by it.
  """
  clustersWatch: ClusterChange!

  """
  Streams cache records as a Kubernetes-style delta watch, parallel to
  `clustersWatch`: the current set as an `Added` burst, then one
  `Added`/`Modified`/`Deleted` change per cache. The client maintains a map keyed by
  `cache.id` and joins each cache onto its cluster by `cache.clusterID`, treating the
  cache whose `serverUid` matches the cluster's `status.server.uid` as the active
  one. Independent of `clustersWatch` (the two streams carry no mutual ordering
  guarantee, so the client tolerates a cache whose cluster has not arrived yet, and
  drops caches orphaned by a cluster deletion).
  """
  clusterCachesWatch: ClusterCacheChange!

  """
  Streams one ClusterCache's discovered kind catalog as a Kubernetes-style delta
  watch (the live counterpart of `clusterDataKinds`, keyed the same way — parent
  cluster `id` + `cacheID`): the current catalog as an `Added` burst on subscribe,
  then one `Added`/`Modified`/`Deleted` change per kind as the sync engine writes
  objects — a kind's per-object `count` is live, so an object write re-emits it as
  `Modified`. The client keys each kind by `apiVersion` + `resource` and rebuilds the
  dashboard resource nav from the accumulated set, so the nav (kinds + counts) tracks
  the cluster in real time. Empty (no frames) until that cache's db is open (a
  never-synced / paused cache streams nothing until it syncs).
  """
  clusterDataKindsWatch(id: ObjectID!, cacheID: ObjectID!): ClusterDataKindChange!

  """
  Streams a cluster's event timeline: the matching runs as a snapshot, then live
  runs (a re-delivered run bumps its count under the same `id`). Optionally
  filtered to one `category`. Independent of clustersWatch, so probe chatter never
  re-emits the cluster (or the list). The client upserts by `id`.
  """
  clusterEventsWatch(id: ObjectID!, category: String): Event!

  """
  Streams a ClusterCache's event timeline, the ClusterCache-kind counterpart of
  `clusterEventsWatch`: the matching runs as a snapshot, then live runs (a
  re-delivered run bumps its count under the same `id`), keyed by the cache's own
  `id` and optionally filtered to one `category` (e.g. `"sync"`). Independent of
  `clusterCachesWatch`, so sync chatter never re-emits the cache. The client upserts
  by `id`.
  """
  clusterCacheEventsWatch(id: ObjectID!, category: String): Event!

  """
  Streams a cluster's reconcile-schedule gauge: the current next-attempt time on
  subscribe, then a fresh value on every (re)schedule (backoff step, retry, poke).
  Independent of clustersWatch — a scheduling change fires no cluster/list watch —
  so this is what keeps a disconnected cluster's "next attempt" countdown live.
  """
  clusterScheduleWatch(id: ObjectID!): Schedule!

  "Streams the assistant's reply token-by-token. Completes after a frame with `done: true`."
  chatStream(input: ChatInput!): ChatChunk!

  "Streams authentication state: the current snapshot, then a fresh one on every sign-in / sign-out / refresh-failure change."
  authStateWatch: AuthState!
}
